field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-341266/field-2/454707892878cf2dd9571a67e15af8dfb75023edf431627663fe6dd748702707" :answer-field/choices ["q-341266/field-2/3a86edc502d0038df0dcd390c378431e5286836bbdd60033424dd2f8510c1df7" "q-341266/field-2/fd0623bef64bcb0a0ce9b59f822de4999c714270edbdc40bbf7ea19c5354d539" "q-341266/field-2/454707892878cf2dd9571a67e15af8dfb75023edf431627663fe6dd748702707" "q-341266/field-2/3f6a3aa25491ef321371576cb221342060d043d2422df9923d1f53fc94cde6cd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-341266" :question/id #uuid "f8ee4544-292e-57b2-955f-27993ad05085" :question/math-academy-id "q-341266" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x+4$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(4+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=x+4$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+4\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((\\pi +4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)-(0+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx))+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(4+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-341266/field-1" "q-341266/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-341266"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21830"] :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x^3 - x + 5\\cos x$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-\\pi,\\pi).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in(-\\pi,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/assets/766b5034dc8f2dd6f3eb8e87f22f5156f58e9ab3aaebd90fa8770d6912d46e67.png)\n\nIn our cases, for $x\\in(-\\pi,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}-(-x)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21833"] :question/problem "$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for $a_0.$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3\\pi }(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }(\\sqrt{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21831"] :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-6x-2$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/assets/ee53aca390d2803fa92f3239dc7548792c6be9e4c05a102fe936aeb9e7ced6d6.png)" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-6x-2$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi (-6x-2) \\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-6x-2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(-6x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{6}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-6\\pi -2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{6}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0-\\frac{6}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\cos(n\\pi)=(-1)^n,$ we have\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim (-2-3\\pi) + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}} \n\\cdot \n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\cos(nx)}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
